UID:7cdf5e7a01edcc6b@role-healthcare-human-services.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261007T230622Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261007
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] US Department of Labor cites Pennsylvania exc
 avation employer for failing to provide trench protection following fatal 
 trench cave-in
DESCRIPTION:CHAMBERSBURG\, PA – The U.S. Department of Labor has cited
  an excavation contractor for willful and serious safety violations after 
 a young worker was fatally injured in a trench collapse. The department
 ’s Occupational Safety and Health Administration opened an investigation
  into Jonas David Wagoner – doing business as J.D. Wagoner Excavating 
 – on April 2\, 2026\, and found workers were installing a storm sewer sy
 stem 12 feet below ground level in a trench that was not adequately protec
 ted from soil collapse. When a portion of the trench's wall failed\, the 
 unrestrained soil engulfed one worker. The agency issued a willful violati
 on for failure to protect workers from cave-ins and two repeat violations 
 for failing to assign a competent person to conduct daily trench inspectio
 ns and removing employees from hazardous conditions.In addition\, the empl
 oyer received three serious violations for failure to provide construction
  hazard prevention training to employees\, establish a safe means for work
 ers to exit the excavation site\, keep materials at least two feet from th
 e edge of an excavation site\, and restrain side materials from becoming r
 oll-in hazards.   OSHA proposed $254\,884 in penalties. The employer ha
 s 15 business days from receiving receipt of its citations and penalties t
 o request an informal conference with OSHA’s area director or contest th
 e findings before the independent Occupational Safety and Health Review Co
 mmission. Please check the OSHA establishment search page periodically for
  any changes in the inspection or penalty status.Visit OSHA’s website to
  learn about keeping workers safe around trenching and excavations. Employ
 ers can also contact the agency for information about OSHA’s compliance
  assistance resources and for free help on complying with OSHA standards.\
 nhttp://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/osha/osha20261007
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/osha/osha20261007
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
